WEBVTT
TITLE Command-Line Arguments in C: argc, the argv Pointer Array and the Null Sentinel — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
The shell splits the command line into words before your program starts.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
The first word is the program's own name, and it counts.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The operating system passes the count and the words to main.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
main is an ordinary function, called by the environment.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Three things were typed after the name; four words arrive.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
argc is the number of strings in argv, not the number of things you typed.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Here argc is 4 while the parameters number 3.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
argc minus one is the parameter count, whenever argc is at least one.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
So a program that wants three parameters tests argc for 4.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
That one extra word is where most marks are lost.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.000
argv is an array whose slots hold pointers, not characters.

12
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Each pointer leads to a separate character array ending in a null.

13
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
argv[0] is the program name, so the first parameter is argv[1].

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The strings have their own lengths, and they need not match.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Reading argv[2][0] gives the first character of the second parameter.

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
The array has one slot more than argc, and the last slot is guaranteed null.

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
argv[argc] shall be a null pointer — that is the standard's word.

18
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
That null is a value you may compare, never a string you may read.

19
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
It exists so a loop can stop on it without knowing the count.

20
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Compare against it freely; dereference it and the program is undefined.

21
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
A counter loop starts at 1 to skip the program name.

22
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
It adds atoi of the words, so ten plus twenty is thirty.

23
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
The plus sign is read too, and atoi turns it into 0.

24
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Starting at 0 instead would add atoi of the program name.

25
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The total hides the bug; only the bound tells the truth.

26
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
The sentinel loop reads a pointer and stops when it is null.

27
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
It visits the same three slots and prints the same 30.

28
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
A bound of argc inclusive also reaches index 4, and that pointer is null.

29
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Handing a null pointer to printf is undefined, not an empty line.

30
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The null is there to be tested, not to be printed.

31
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Run with nothing after the name, and argc is 1 — never 0.

32
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
argv[1] then holds the null pointer, so there is no string there.

33
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
atoi(argv[1]) on that run dereferences null: undefined.

34
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
The guard is a test of argc before the read, not after.

35
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
argc may be 0 in the standard's wording, and then argv[0] is not the name.

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Every parameter arrives as text, so a number has to be converted.

37
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
atoi reads the longest prefix that forms a number and stops.

38
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
atoi cannot report failure: abc and 0 both give 0.

39
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
strtol takes an end pointer, and the end pointer is the report.

40
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Use strtol when zero and nonsense must be told apart.

41
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
char *argv[] and char **argv declare the same parameter.

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
In a parameter list an array type is rewritten as a pointer.

43
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
So sizeof(argv) inside main is the size of a pointer, not the array.

44
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
The count is argc; the pointer width tells you nothing about it.

45
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
sizeof(*argv) is a pointer, and sizeof(**argv) is one byte.

46
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Guaranteed: argc is nonnegative, and the slot at argc is null.

47
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Guaranteed: argv and the strings it points to may be modified.

48
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Still not allowed: writing past a string's own length.

49
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Left open: where the strings live, and what argv[0] holds.

50
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Five steps: check argc, start at 1, stop at argc or null, convert, report.

